The temperature of hot chocolate is recorded as it cools down. The data shows that the temperature cools according to the equation:
\[
T=82(0.87)^{t}+16
\]
where \(T\) is the temperature of the hot chocolate, in degrees Celsius after \(t\) minutes and \(t\) is the time, in minutes, after the hot chocolate is made.

In order to avoid burns, hot chocolate should not be served at a temperature higher than 71°C. Determine, algebraically, the amount of time it will take the hot chocolate to reach this temperature.

\section*{Solution}
\[
\begin{aligned}
71 & =82(0.87)^{t}+16 \\
55 & =82(0.87)^{t} \\
\frac{55}{82} & =0.87^{t}
\end{aligned}
\]
\(\log \left(\frac{55}{82}\right)=t \log (0.87)\) \(\frac{1}{2}\) mark for applying logarithms 1 mark for power law
\[
\begin{aligned}
